course: dictionary
lesson: 6162
title: Mine: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. The word mine is a good example. «That book is mine» uses mine to show who owns something. Here mine means belonging to me.
explain: The same word can also be a noun or a verb. «The miners went down the mine each morning» uses mine as a noun, a deep hole or tunnels in the ground. «They mine coal from the hills» uses mine as a verb, meaning to dig something out of the ground.
explain: Always check the words around the word. «Never go into old mines, caves or tunnels; they can collapse» is about dangerous holes in the ground, so mine means a deep hole or tunnels, not something that belongs to you. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: Mine can be a pronoun: «That book is mine» means it belongs to me.
fact: Mine can be a noun: a mine is a deep hole or tunnels where people dig out coal, gold, metals or stones.
fact: Mine can be a verb: to mine means to dig coal, metals or stones out of the ground.
fact: A mine can also be a bomb hidden in the ground or the sea that explodes when touched.
fact: «A mine of information» means a rich source of facts. Here mine means a place full of something valuable, not a hole in the ground.
exercise: «That book is mine.» What does mine mean here? | A bomb hidden in the sea | To dig coal out of the ground | *Belonging to me | A deep hole in the ground
exercise: «She works in a coal mine far under the ground.» What is a mine in this sentence? | A type of tall tower | A bird's nest in a tall tree | A kind of small boat | *A deep hole or tunnels where coal is dug out
exercise: «They mine gold from the rock.» What does mine mean here? | To count carefully | *To dig out of the ground | To explode | To belong to me
test: «Never go into old mines, caves or tunnels; they can collapse or be full of bad air.» Why is it dangerous? | They are full of toys | Mines are too bright to look at | Mines are always warm | *They can collapse or fill with bad air
test: Jade says, «Dad is a mine of information about rocks.» What does she mean? | He digs holes for a living | *He knows a great deal, so he is a rich source of facts | He has a lot of coal in the shed | He is always getting lost
exam: «That pen is mine, not yours.» Which word is the pronoun that shows who owns the pen? | that | *mine | pen | yours
